{
 "preset": "fermi",
 "topic": "Where is everybody? The Fermi Paradox",
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Look up on a clear night and you can see a few thousand stars.",
   "start": 0.8,
   "end": 6.416455696202531
  },
  {
   "text": "Our galaxy alone holds around two hundred billion of them.",
   "start": 7.166455696202531,
   "end": 11.263924050632912
  },
  {
   "text": "Many have planets, and some of those planets orbit where water can stay liquid.",
   "start": 12.01392405063291,
   "end": 17.63037974683544
  },
  {
   "text": "Even if life starts on only a tiny fraction of them, the galaxy should be crowded.",
   "start": 18.380379746835445,
   "end": 24.756329113924053
  },
  {
   "text": "A civilization slightly older than ours could have spread across it in a few million years.",
   "start": 25.506329113924053,
   "end": 31.882278481012662
  },
  {
   "text": "So where is everybody?",
   "start": 32.63227848101266,
   "end": 34.45126582278481
  },
  {
   "text": "This is the Fermi Paradox.",
   "start": 35.63227848101266,
   "end": 37.83101265822785
  },
  {
   "text": "One answer is a Great Filter: a step so hard that almost nothing gets past it.",
   "start": 39.03227848101266,
   "end": 45.40822784810126
  },
  {
   "text": "Maybe it is behind us, and simple life almost never becomes complex.",
   "start": 46.15822784810127,
   "end": 51.015189873417725
  },
  {
   "text": "Or maybe it lies ahead, waiting for every civilization that reaches our level.",
   "start": 51.765189873417725,
   "end": 57.00189873417722
  },
  {
   "text": "Perhaps they are out there, but too far apart, too quiet, or not interested in us.",
   "start": 57.75189873417722,
   "end": 64.12784810126583
  },
  {
   "text": "Or perhaps we really are among the first.",
   "start": 64.87784810126581,
   "end": 68.21582278481011
  },
  {
   "text": "Either way, the silence says something about our own future.",
   "start": 68.96582278481011,
   "end": 73.06329113924049
  }
 ],
